{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CRo8ezaukFNps3iUu9SLEs","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel04.1-uc001-ls-extended\n     2→title: \"List directory contents via ls (extended flags)\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator runs the Go ls binary with extended flags (-C, -x, -t, -S, -r, -U, -v, -i,\n     6→  -s, -n, -F, -R) on a fixture directory. The differential testing harness runs both the Go\n     7→  binary and the Homebrew reference binary (gls from coreutils) with the same arguments and\n     8→  environment, applies an LsExtendedNormalizer (which extends the core LsNormalizer with\n     9→  mtime replacement across recursive subdirectory listings), and confirms that the normalized\n    10→  outputs are identical. All tests use LC_ALL=C and --color=never. Fixture files have\n    11→  controlled sizes, mtimes, and permissions to ensure deterministic sort order.\n    12→\n    13→actor: Human Operator\n    14→\n    15→trigger: |\n    16→  The operator has completed prd010-ls-extended.yaml (extended flags PRD) and the core\n    17→  ls PRD (prd008-ls.yaml) and invokes do-work to produce a passing differential test for\n    18→  cmd/ls extended flags.\n    19→\n    20→flow:\n    21→  - id: F1\n    22→    step: |\n    23→      The operator runs the Go ls binary with extended flags on a fixture directory with\n    24→      controlled file sizes, mtimes, and types. Example: ./bin/ls -lR --color=never testdata/ls-fixture/\n    25→  - id: F2\n    26→    step: |\n    27→      cmd/ls reads directory entries and obtains file metadata via pkg/sys.Stat for sort-by-time\n    28→      (-t), sort-by-size (-S), inode display (-i), and block count display (-s). It formats\n    29→      multi-column output via pkg/format.Columns for -C and -x modes.\n    30→  - id: F3\n    31→    step: |\n    32→      For -R (recursive), cmd/ls descends into each subdirectory and prints a \"path:\" header\n    33→      followed by the directory contents in the active format mode. Each subdirectory listing\n    34→      applies the same flags (sort, filter, format, metadata).\n    35→  - id: F4\n    36→    step: |\n    37→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/ls and gls with\n    38→      the same arguments, environment (LC_ALL=C, --color=never), and working directory.\n    39→  - id: F5\n    40→    step: |\n    41→      The harness applies the LsExtendedNormalizer to both outputs. This normalizer replaces\n    42→      all mtime fields (in long-format output and in recursive subdirectory listings) with the\n    43→      placeholder \"MTIME\". Inode numbers and block counts are not normalized; both binaries\n    44→      stat the same files on the same filesystem, producing identical values.\n    45→  - id: F6\n    46→    step: |\n    47→      The harness compares the normalized outputs byte-for-byte. Both binaries must produce\n    48→      identical sort order, column layout, metadata values, type indicators, and recursive\n    49→      subdirectory structure.\n    50→  - id: F7\n    51→    step: |\n    52→      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n    53→      triggering args, both raw outputs, both normalized outputs, and both exit codes.\n    54→\n    55→touchpoints:\n    56→  - T1: \"cmd/ls — multi-column layout, sort order, metadata display, classify, recursive (prd010-ls-extended R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6)\"\n    57→  - T2: \"pkg/sys — TerminalWidth, Stat/Lstat for mtime, size, inode, blocks\"\n    58→  - T3: \"pkg/format — Columns for -C/-x, FileTypeColor, HumanSize for -s -h\"\n    59→  - T4: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, LsExtendedNormalizer\"\n    60→\n    61→success_criteria:\n    62→  - id: S1\n    63→    criterion: |\n    64→      -C produces multi-column output even when stdout is piped, matching gls -C output on\n    65→      the same fixture with a fixed column width.\n    66→  - id: S2\n    67→    criterion: |\n    68→      -x produces horizontal-sorted multi-column output, matching gls -x.\n    69→  - id: S3\n    70→    criterion: |\n    71→      -t sorts entries by modification time (newest first) on a fixture with files of known\n    72→      distinct mtimes, matching gls -t output.\n    73→  - id: S4\n    74→    criterion: |\n    75→      -S sorts entries by size (largest first), matching gls -S output.\n    76→  - id: S5\n    77→    criterion: |\n    78→      -r reverses the sort order, matching gls -r output.\n    79→  - id: S6\n    80→    criterion: |\n    81→      -i prepends inode numbers to each entry, matching gls -i on the same fixture.\n    82→  - id: S7\n    83→    criterion: |\n    84→      -s prepends block counts to each entry, matching gls -s on the same fixture.\n    85→  - id: S8\n    86→    criterion: |\n    87→      -n produces long format with numeric UID/GID instead of names, matching gls -n after\n    88→      MTIME normalization.\n    89→  - id: S9\n    90→    criterion: |\n    91→      -F appends type indicators (/, *, @, |, =) matching gls -F on a fixture with a\n    92→      directory, executable, symlink, and named pipe.\n    93→  - id: S10\n    94→    criterion: |\n    95→      -R recursively lists subdirectories with \"path:\" headers and blank-line separators,\n    96→      matching gls -R after MTIME normalization.\n    97→  - id: S11\n    98→    criterion: |\n    99→      The differential test passes for all extended flags: -C, -x, -t, -S, -r, -U, -v, -i,\n   100→      -s, -n, -F, -R, and combinations (-Rl, -tr, -SF).\n   101→\n   102→out_of_scope:\n   103→  - -v (version sort) test uses a simple fixture with files named file1, file2, file10;\n   104→    full strverscmp edge cases are not exercised in the differential test.\n   105→  - -U (unsorted) output order depends on filesystem directory order; both binaries produce\n   106→    the same order when reading the same directory, so comparison is valid but not a test\n   107→    of Go-specific behavior.\n   108→  - SIGWINCH and terminal resize behavior during -R recursive listing is not testable in the\n   109→    differential harness.\n   110→\n   111→test_suite: test-rel04.1\n   112→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5db64dcf-77d9-43f9-86cc-418e9c0d9211","uuid":"183a8589-2900-46ac-b2d1-c95bb0a3fadf","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/247/docs/specs/use-cases/rel04.1-uc001-ls-extended.yaml","content":"id: rel04.1-uc001-ls-extended\ntitle: \"List directory contents via ls (extended flags)\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator runs the Go ls binary with extended flags (-C, -x, -t, -S, -r, -U, -v, -i,\n  -s, -n, -F, -R) on a fixture directory. The differential testing harness runs both the Go\n  binary and the Homebrew reference binary (gls from coreutils) with the same arguments and\n  environment, applies an LsExtendedNormalizer (which extends the core LsNormalizer with\n  mtime replacement across recursive subdirectory listings), and confirms that the normalized\n  outputs are identical. All tests use LC_ALL=C and --color=never. Fixture files have\n  controlled sizes, mtimes, and permissions to ensure deterministic sort order.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed prd010-ls-extended.yaml (extended flags PRD) and the core\n  ls PRD (prd008-ls.yaml) and invokes do-work to produce a passing differential test for\n  cmd/ls extended flags.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs the Go ls binary with extended flags on a fixture directory with\n      controlled file sizes, mtimes, and types. Example: ./bin/ls -lR --color=never testdata/ls-fixture/\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      cmd/ls reads directory entries and obtains file metadata via pkg/sys.Stat for sort-by-time\n      (-t), sort-by-size (-S), inode display (-i), and block count display (-s). It formats\n      multi-column output via pkg/format.Columns for -C and -x modes.\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      For -R (recursive), cmd/ls descends into each subdirectory and prints a \"path:\" header\n      followed by the directory contents in the active format mode. Each subdirectory listing\n      applies the same flags (sort, filter, format, metadata).\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/ls and gls with\n      the same arguments, environment (LC_ALL=C, --color=never), and working directory.\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The harness applies the LsExtendedNormalizer to both outputs. This normalizer replaces\n      all mtime fields (in long-format output and in recursive subdirectory listings) with the\n      placeholder \"MTIME\". Inode numbers and block counts are not normalized; both binaries\n      stat the same files on the same filesystem, producing identical values.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      The harness compares the normalized outputs byte-for-byte. Both binaries must produce\n      identical sort order, column layout, metadata values, type indicators, and recursive\n      subdirectory structure.\n  - id: F7\n    step: |\n      If the outputs match, the test passes. If they diverge, the harness reports the\n      triggering args, both raw outputs, both normalized outputs, and both exit codes.\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"cmd/ls — multi-column layout, sort order, metadata display, classify, recursive (prd010-ls-extended R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/sys — TerminalWidth, Stat/Lstat for mtime, size, inode, blocks\"\n  - T3: \"pkg/format — Columns for -C/-x, FileTypeColor, HumanSize for -s -h\"\n  - T4: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, LsExtendedNormalizer\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: |\n      -C produces multi-column output even when stdout is piped, matching gls -C output on\n      the same fixture with a fixed column width.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: |\n      -x produces horizontal-sorted multi-column output, matching gls -x.\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: |\n      -t sorts entries by modification time (newest first) on a fixture with files of known\n      distinct mtimes, matching gls -t output.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: |\n      -S sorts entries by size (largest first), matching gls -S output.\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: |\n      -r reverses the sort order, matching gls -r output.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: |\n      -i prepends inode numbers to each entry, matching gls -i on the same fixture.\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: |\n      -s prepends block counts to each entry, matching gls -s on the same fixture.\n  - id: S8\n    criterion: |\n      -n produces long format with numeric UID/GID instead of names, matching gls -n after\n      MTIME normalization.\n  - id: S9\n    criterion: |\n      -F appends type indicators (/, *, @, |, =) matching gls -F on a fixture with a\n      directory, executable, symlink, and named pipe.\n  - id: S10\n    criterion: |\n      -R recursively lists subdirectories with \"path:\" headers and blank-line separators,\n      matching gls -R after MTIME normalization.\n  - id: S11\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test passes for all extended flags: -C, -x, -t, -S, -r, -U, -v, -i,\n      -s, -n, -F, -R, and combinations (-Rl, -tr, -SF).\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - -v (version sort) test uses a simple fixture with files named file1, file2, file10;\n    full strverscmp edge cases are not exercised in the differential test.\n  - -U (unsorted) output order depends on filesystem directory order; both binaries produce\n    the same order when reading the same directory, so comparison is valid but not a test\n    of Go-specific behavior.\n  - SIGWINCH and terminal resize behavior during -R recursive listing is not testable in the\n    differential harness.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel04.1\n","numLines":112,"startLine":1,"totalLines":112}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018JJQ5TsPeEHv3254LFKoEP","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd008-ls\n     2→title: \"cmd/ls: Core File Listing\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  ls is the most frequently used Unix utility, and its behavior varies between macOS BSD ls\n     6→  and GNU ls in column layout, color handling, long-format field widths, and human-readable\n     7→  size formatting. Scripts and interactive shells depend on exact output format for parsing\n     8→  and display. A Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gls,\n     9→  installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag combinations, verified by\n    10→  differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    11→\n    12→  ls is the primary consumer of both pkg/sys and pkg/format. It requires terminal-width\n    13→  queries for multi-column layout, extended file metadata for long format, signal handling\n    14→  for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH, column alignment for tabular output, ANSI color for file-type\n    15→  colorization, and human-readable size conversion. This PRD covers the core flag subset\n    16→  for rel02.0; extended flags are deferred to prd010-ls-extended.\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: List directory contents in multi-column, single-column, or long format, matching GNU ls output layout.\n    20→  - G2: Support filter flags (-a, -A, -d) to control which entries appear in the listing.\n    21→  - G3: Support colorized output via --color with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n    22→  - G4: Support human-readable file sizes (-h) in long format, matching GNU ls -lh output.\n    23→  - G5: Produce correct exit codes (0, 1, 2) matching GNU ls exit-code contract.\n    24→\n    25→requirements:\n    26→  R1:\n    27→    title: Default listing (multi-column and single-column)\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R1.1: |\n    30→          When stdout is a TTY and no format flag is given, must produce multi-column output sorted vertically (down columns, then across). Terminal width is obtained by calling pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() (int, error) — returns the current terminal column count via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl on stdout; returns an error when stdout is not a terminal. Entries are laid out by calling pkg/format.Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string — distributes entries into the maximum number of columns fitting within termWidth, with column widths computed as per-column maxima of entry lengths.\n    31→      - R1.2: When stdout is not a TTY (pipe, file redirect) and no format flag is given, must produce single-column output with one entry per line. This matches GNU ls behavior (ls.c detects stdout is not a TTY and sets format to single-column).\n    32→      - R1.3: Must sort entries in the C locale order (LC_COLLATE=C). Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent sort divergence.\n    33→      - R1.4: Must not list entries whose names start with \".\" unless -a or -A is given. This is the default filter behavior matching GNU ls.\n    34→\n    35→  R2:\n    36→    title: Single-column mode (-1) and long format (-l)\n    37→    items:\n    38→      - R2.1: -1 must produce single-column output with one entry per line regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. This overrides the default multi-column layout.\n    39→      - R2.2: |\n    40→          -l flag skeleton and permission string. When -l is given, produce long-format output. Field order (left to right): permissions (10 chars), nlink, owner, group, size, mtime, name. Fields separated by a single space. Numeric fields (nlink, size) are right-aligned in columns wide enough for the largest value in the listing.\n    41→\n    42→          Permission string algorithm (10 characters):\n    43→          - Position 0 (file type): 'd' for directory (fi.Mode&os.ModeDir != 0), 'l' for symlink (fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0), 'c' for char device (fi.Mode&os.ModeDevice != 0 && fi.Mode&os.ModeCharDevice != 0), 'b' for block device (fi.Mode&os.ModeDevice != 0 && fi.Mode&os.ModeCharDevice == 0), 'p' for FIFO (fi.Mode&os.ModeNamedPipe != 0), 's' for socket (fi.Mode&os.ModeSocket != 0), '-' for regular file.\n    44→          - Positions 1-3 (owner rwx): 'r'/'- ' for bit 0o400, 'w'/'-' for 0o200, 'x'/'-' for 0o100. If setuid (fi.Mode&os.ModeSetuid != 0): replace 'x' with 's', or replace '-' with 'S'.\n    45→          - Positions 4-6 (group rwx): bits 0o040, 0o020, 0o010. If setgid (fi.Mode&os.ModeSetgid != 0): replace 'x' with 's', or replace '-' with 'S'.\n    46→          - Positions 7-9 (other rwx): bits 0o004, 0o002, 0o001. If sticky (fi.Mode&os.ModeSticky != 0): replace 'x' with 't', or replace '-' with 'T'.\n    47→      - R2.3: |\n    48→          File metadata via pkg/sys.Lstat. Obtain metadata for each listed entry by calling pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — does not follow symbolic links; returns the symlink's own metadata when path is a symlink.\n    49→\n    50→          sys.FileInfo struct (embedded here for reference):\n    51→            type FileInfo struct {\n    52→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits\n    53→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    54→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    55→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    56→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    57→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    58→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    59→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    60→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    61→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    62→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time\n    63→                Sys     os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo\n    64→            }\n    65→\n    66→          In long format: print fi.Nlink (uint64) right-aligned; print fi.Size (int64, bytes) right-aligned. Both columns sized to the widest value across all listed entries.\n    67→      - R2.4: |\n    68→          Owner and group name resolution in long format. Resolve owner name by calling os/user.LookupId(strconv.Itoa(int(fi.Uid))); on success use u.Username; on error (user not found or lookup fails) fall back to strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Uid), 10). Resolve group name by calling os/user.LookupGroupId(strconv.Itoa(int(fi.Gid))); on success use g.Name; on error fall back to strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Gid), 10). Owner and group fields are left-padded to column width for alignment.\n    69→      - R2.5: |\n    70→          Modification time formatting in long format. Use fi.ModTime (time.Time from sys.FileInfo). If time.Since(fi.ModTime) < 6*30*24*time.Hour (approximately six months), format as fi.ModTime.Format(\"Jan _2 15:04\"). Otherwise format as fi.ModTime.Format(\"Jan _2  2006\"). Both patterns produce a 12-character field.\n    71→      - R2.6: |\n    72→          Total block count line and symlink display. Before the directory listing, print \"total N\\n\" where N = sum(fi.Blocks for all listed entries) / 2. The division converts 512-byte st_blocks units to 1K-block units matching GNU ls. N is a plain integer (no trailing suffix) unless -h is active (see R5).\n    73→\n    74→          Symlink display: when fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0, read the link target with os.Readlink(path) and append \" -> \" + target to the entry name field. Use Lstat (not Stat) so the symlink's own fi.Size and fi.Nlink are used, not the target's.\n    75→\n    76→  R3:\n    77→    title: Filter flags (-a, -A, -d)\n    78→    items:\n    79→      - R3.1: -a must include entries whose names start with \".\" including the special entries \".\" and \"..\" in the listing.\n    80→      - R3.2: -A must include entries whose names start with \".\" except for \".\" and \"..\". -A is the \"almost all\" filter.\n    81→      - R3.3: -d must list directory entries themselves rather than their contents. When a directory is given as an argument with -d, must print the directory name (or its long-format entry with -l) without descending into it.\n    82→      - R3.4: When both -a and -A are given, the last one on the command line takes precedence, matching GNU ls behavior.\n    83→\n    84→  R4:\n    85→    title: Color output (--color)\n    86→    items:\n    87→      - R4.1: Must support --color=always, --color=auto, and --color=never. When --color is given without a value, it must default to \"always\".\n    88→      - R4.2: |\n    89→          --color=auto must colorize output only when stdout is a TTY. TTY detection: call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(os.Stdout) — returns true when os.Stdout's file descriptor is a terminal (detected via golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize with TIOCGWINSZ), false otherwise. When stdout is not a TTY, --color=auto must behave like --color=never.\n    90→      - R4.3: |\n    91→          Colorized output uses GNU LS_COLORS default color assignments via pkg/format. Call pkg/format.FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string to obtain the ANSI escape sequence for a given file type, and pkg/format.Reset() string for the reset sequence. Executable detection: fi.Mode&0o111 != 0 (any execute bit set). Each entry name is wrapped as: colorCode + name + resetCode. When color is suppressed, both codes are empty strings so no ANSI sequences appear in output.\n    92→\n    93→          Set the process-global color mode based on the --color flag value: call pkg/format.SetColorEnabled(true) for --color=always, pkg/format.SetColorEnabled(false) for --color=never. For --color=auto, call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(os.Stdout.Fd()) and pass the result to pkg/format.SetColorEnabled. When color is disabled, pkg/format.FileTypeColor returns empty strings.\n    94→      - R4.4: When --color=never or when color is suppressed (non-TTY with auto), must not emit any ANSI escape sequences in the output.\n    95→\n    96→  R5:\n    97→    title: Human-readable sizes (-h)\n    98→    items:\n    99→      - R5.1: |\n   100→          -h must only take effect with -l (long format). When -h is given with -l, file sizes (fi.Size int64) must be displayed via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with opts.Binary = true (1024-base units, suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E). Without -l, -h must have no visible effect on output.\n   101→      - R5.2: -h must also apply to the \"total N\" block count line in long format. When -h is active, convert the total block count (sum(fi.Blocks)/2 as int64) to a human-readable string using the same binary algorithm before printing \"total X\\n\".\n   102→      - R5.3: Without -l, -h must have no visible effect on output.\n   103→\n   104→  R6:\n   105→    title: Exit codes\n   106→    items:\n   107→      - R6.1: Must exit 0 when all listed paths are accessed and output is written successfully.\n   108→      - R6.2: Must exit 1 when a minor problem occurs, such as failing to access a file or directory given as an argument (e.g., permission denied, file not found). Must still list the remaining accessible entries before exiting. Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each inaccessible entry.\n   109→      - R6.3: Must exit 2 when a serious problem occurs, such as an invalid command-line option. This matches GNU ls (ls.c:5638 exit(status) where status is set to EXIT_FAILURE=2 for bad options at ls.c:2026).\n   110→\n   111→  R7:\n   112→    title: Signal handling\n   113→    items:\n   114→      - R7.1: |\n   115→          Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup so that piping ls output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message. Implementation: call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() — this uses signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; a dedicated goroutine calls os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU ls behavior).\n   116→      - R7.2: Must install a SIGWINCH handler via pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that re-queries the terminal width and updates the column layout. pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize registers a SIGWINCH listener that calls pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width; multiple callbacks may be registered. This allows ls to adapt column layout if the terminal is resized during a long listing.\n   117→\n   118→non_goals:\n   119→  - -C (force multi-column), -x (sort across), -t (sort by mtime), -S (sort by size), -r (reverse sort), -U (unsorted), -v (version sort), -i (inode number), -s (block size), -n (numeric UID/GID), -R (recursive), -F (classify with indicator) are deferred to prd010-ls-extended.\n   120→  - --hyperlink (terminal hyperlinks) is out of scope for this project.\n   121→  - --dired (Emacs dired mode) is out of scope.\n   122→  - -Z (SELinux security context) is out of scope; this project does not target SELinux.\n   123→  - --quoting-style (filename quoting) is out of scope for this release.\n   124→  - --block-size unit suffixes beyond -h are out of scope for this release.\n   125→  - LS_COLORS environment variable parsing is out of scope; we use GNU default colors only (prd003-format non-goals).\n   126→  - ACL (access control list) display in long format (the \"+\" indicator) is out of scope.\n   127→\n   128→acceptance_criteria:\n   129→  - \"AC1: Default mode comparison -- gls and go run ./cmd/ls on a test directory produce identical multi-column output when run in a pseudo-TTY with the same terminal width. Differential test must set LC_ALL=C and --color=never.\"\n   130→  - \"AC2: Single-column comparison -- gls -1 and go run ./cmd/ls -1 produce identical output on the same test directory.\"\n   131→  - \"AC3: Long format comparison -- gls -l and go run ./cmd/ls -l produce identical output on the same test directory. Because mtime display depends on wall clock, differential tests must normalize the mtime column by replacing it with a fixed placeholder (e.g., \\\"MTIME\\\") before comparison. Because owner/group names may vary, tests must use a fixture where files are owned by the test user, or normalize owner/group fields.\"\n   132→  - \"AC4: Color suppression -- gls --color=auto and go run ./cmd/ls --color=auto produce no ANSI escape sequences when stdout is piped (not a TTY).\"\n   133→  - \"AC5: Human-readable sizes -- gls -lh and go run ./cmd/ls -lh produce identical size strings on a set of test files with known sizes (0 bytes, 1023 bytes, 1024 bytes, 1536 bytes, 1048576 bytes, 1073741824 bytes).\"\n   134→  - \"AC6: Filter flags -- gls -a, gls -A, and gls -d produce identical output to the Go binary with the same flags on a test directory containing dotfiles and subdirectories.\"\n   135→  - \"AC7: Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent path argument and exits 2 when given an invalid option, matching gls behavior.\"\n   136→  - \"AC8: cmd/ls compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n   137→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5db64dcf-77d9-43f9-86cc-418e9c0d9211","uuid":"1574db47-47ea-441c-bd65-dead000c7a6d","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/247/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd008-ls.yaml","content":"id: prd008-ls\ntitle: \"cmd/ls: Core File Listing\"\n\nproblem: |\n  ls is the most frequently used Unix utility, and its behavior varies between macOS BSD ls\n  and GNU ls in column layout, color handling, long-format field widths, and human-readable\n  size formatting. Scripts and interactive shells depend on exact output format for parsing\n  and display. A Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gls,\n  installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag combinations, verified by\n  differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\n  ls is the primary consumer of both pkg/sys and pkg/format. It requires terminal-width\n  queries for multi-column layout, extended file metadata for long format, signal handling\n  for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH, column alignment for tabular output, ANSI color for file-type\n  colorization, and human-readable size conversion. This PRD covers the core flag subset\n  for rel02.0; extended flags are deferred to prd010-ls-extended.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: List directory contents in multi-column, single-column, or long format, matching GNU ls output layout.\n  - G2: Support filter flags (-a, -A, -d) to control which entries appear in the listing.\n  - G3: Support colorized output via --color with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n  - G4: Support human-readable file sizes (-h) in long format, matching GNU ls -lh output.\n  - G5: Produce correct exit codes (0, 1, 2) matching GNU ls exit-code contract.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default listing (multi-column and single-column)\n    items:\n      - R1.1: |\n          When stdout is a TTY and no format flag is given, must produce multi-column output sorted vertically (down columns, then across). Terminal width is obtained by calling pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() (int, error) — returns the current terminal column count via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl on stdout; returns an error when stdout is not a terminal. Entries are laid out by calling pkg/format.Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string — distributes entries into the maximum number of columns fitting within termWidth, with column widths computed as per-column maxima of entry lengths.\n      - R1.2: When stdout is not a TTY (pipe, file redirect) and no format flag is given, must produce single-column output with one entry per line. This matches GNU ls behavior (ls.c detects stdout is not a TTY and sets format to single-column).\n      - R1.3: Must sort entries in the C locale order (LC_COLLATE=C). Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent sort divergence.\n      - R1.4: Must not list entries whose names start with \".\" unless -a or -A is given. This is the default filter behavior matching GNU ls.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Single-column mode (-1) and long format (-l)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: -1 must produce single-column output with one entry per line regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. This overrides the default multi-column layout.\n      - R2.2: |\n          -l flag skeleton and permission string. When -l is given, produce long-format output. Field order (left to right): permissions (10 chars), nlink, owner, group, size, mtime, name. Fields separated by a single space. Numeric fields (nlink, size) are right-aligned in columns wide enough for the largest value in the listing.\n\n          Permission string algorithm (10 characters):\n          - Position 0 (file type): 'd' for directory (fi.Mode&os.ModeDir != 0), 'l' for symlink (fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0), 'c' for char device (fi.Mode&os.ModeDevice != 0 && fi.Mode&os.ModeCharDevice != 0), 'b' for block device (fi.Mode&os.ModeDevice != 0 && fi.Mode&os.ModeCharDevice == 0), 'p' for FIFO (fi.Mode&os.ModeNamedPipe != 0), 's' for socket (fi.Mode&os.ModeSocket != 0), '-' for regular file.\n          - Positions 1-3 (owner rwx): 'r'/'- ' for bit 0o400, 'w'/'-' for 0o200, 'x'/'-' for 0o100. If setuid (fi.Mode&os.ModeSetuid != 0): replace 'x' with 's', or replace '-' with 'S'.\n          - Positions 4-6 (group rwx): bits 0o040, 0o020, 0o010. If setgid (fi.Mode&os.ModeSetgid != 0): replace 'x' with 's', or replace '-' with 'S'.\n          - Positions 7-9 (other rwx): bits 0o004, 0o002, 0o001. If sticky (fi.Mode&os.ModeSticky != 0): replace 'x' with 't', or replace '-' with 'T'.\n      - R2.3: |\n          File metadata via pkg/sys.Lstat. Obtain metadata for each listed entry by calling pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — does not follow symbolic links; returns the symlink's own metadata when path is a symlink.\n\n          sys.FileInfo struct (embedded here for reference):\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time\n                Sys     os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo\n            }\n\n          In long format: print fi.Nlink (uint64) right-aligned; print fi.Size (int64, bytes) right-aligned. Both columns sized to the widest value across all listed entries.\n      - R2.4: |\n          Owner and group name resolution in long format. Resolve owner name by calling os/user.LookupId(strconv.Itoa(int(fi.Uid))); on success use u.Username; on error (user not found or lookup fails) fall back to strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Uid), 10). Resolve group name by calling os/user.LookupGroupId(strconv.Itoa(int(fi.Gid))); on success use g.Name; on error fall back to strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Gid), 10). Owner and group fields are left-padded to column width for alignment.\n      - R2.5: |\n          Modification time formatting in long format. Use fi.ModTime (time.Time from sys.FileInfo). If time.Since(fi.ModTime) < 6*30*24*time.Hour (approximately six months), format as fi.ModTime.Format(\"Jan _2 15:04\"). Otherwise format as fi.ModTime.Format(\"Jan _2  2006\"). Both patterns produce a 12-character field.\n      - R2.6: |\n          Total block count line and symlink display. Before the directory listing, print \"total N\\n\" where N = sum(fi.Blocks for all listed entries) / 2. The division converts 512-byte st_blocks units to 1K-block units matching GNU ls. N is a plain integer (no trailing suffix) unless -h is active (see R5).\n\n          Symlink display: when fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0, read the link target with os.Readlink(path) and append \" -> \" + target to the entry name field. Use Lstat (not Stat) so the symlink's own fi.Size and fi.Nlink are used, not the target's.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Filter flags (-a, -A, -d)\n    items:\n      - R3.1: -a must include entries whose names start with \".\" including the special entries \".\" and \"..\" in the listing.\n      - R3.2: -A must include entries whose names start with \".\" except for \".\" and \"..\". -A is the \"almost all\" filter.\n      - R3.3: -d must list directory entries themselves rather than their contents. When a directory is given as an argument with -d, must print the directory name (or its long-format entry with -l) without descending into it.\n      - R3.4: When both -a and -A are given, the last one on the command line takes precedence, matching GNU ls behavior.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Color output (--color)\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must support --color=always, --color=auto, and --color=never. When --color is given without a value, it must default to \"always\".\n      - R4.2: |\n          --color=auto must colorize output only when stdout is a TTY. TTY detection: call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(os.Stdout) — returns true when os.Stdout's file descriptor is a terminal (detected via golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize with TIOCGWINSZ), false otherwise. When stdout is not a TTY, --color=auto must behave like --color=never.\n      - R4.3: |\n          Colorized output uses GNU LS_COLORS default color assignments via pkg/format. Call pkg/format.FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string to obtain the ANSI escape sequence for a given file type, and pkg/format.Reset() string for the reset sequence. Executable detection: fi.Mode&0o111 != 0 (any execute bit set). Each entry name is wrapped as: colorCode + name + resetCode. When color is suppressed, both codes are empty strings so no ANSI sequences appear in output.\n\n          Set the process-global color mode based on the --color flag value: call pkg/format.SetColorEnabled(true) for --color=always, pkg/format.SetColorEnabled(false) for --color=never. For --color=auto, call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(os.Stdout.Fd()) and pass the result to pkg/format.SetColorEnabled. When color is disabled, pkg/format.FileTypeColor returns empty strings.\n      - R4.4: When --color=never or when color is suppressed (non-TTY with auto), must not emit any ANSI escape sequences in the output.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Human-readable sizes (-h)\n    items:\n      - R5.1: |\n          -h must only take effect with -l (long format). When -h is given with -l, file sizes (fi.Size int64) must be displayed via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with opts.Binary = true (1024-base units, suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E). Without -l, -h must have no visible effect on output.\n      - R5.2: -h must also apply to the \"total N\" block count line in long format. When -h is active, convert the total block count (sum(fi.Blocks)/2 as int64) to a human-readable string using the same binary algorithm before printing \"total X\\n\".\n      - R5.3: Without -l, -h must have no visible effect on output.\n\n  R6:\n    title: Exit codes\n    items:\n      - R6.1: Must exit 0 when all listed paths are accessed and output is written successfully.\n      - R6.2: Must exit 1 when a minor problem occurs, such as failing to access a file or directory given as an argument (e.g., permission denied, file not found). Must still list the remaining accessible entries before exiting. Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each inaccessible entry.\n      - R6.3: Must exit 2 when a serious problem occurs, such as an invalid command-line option. This matches GNU ls (ls.c:5638 exit(status) where status is set to EXIT_FAILURE=2 for bad options at ls.c:2026).\n\n  R7:\n    title: Signal handling\n    items:\n      - R7.1: |\n          Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup so that piping ls output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message. Implementation: call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() — this uses signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; a dedicated goroutine calls os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU ls behavior).\n      - R7.2: Must install a SIGWINCH handler via pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that re-queries the terminal width and updates the column layout. pkg/sys.OnTerminalResize registers a SIGWINCH listener that calls pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width; multiple callbacks may be registered. This allows ls to adapt column layout if the terminal is resized during a long listing.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - -C (force multi-column), -x (sort across), -t (sort by mtime), -S (sort by size), -r (reverse sort), -U (unsorted), -v (version sort), -i (inode number), -s (block size), -n (numeric UID/GID), -R (recursive), -F (classify with indicator) are deferred to prd010-ls-extended.\n  - --hyperlink (terminal hyperlinks) is out of scope for this project.\n  - --dired (Emacs dired mode) is out of scope.\n  - -Z (SELinux security context) is out of scope; this project does not target SELinux.\n  - --quoting-style (filename quoting) is out of scope for this release.\n  - --block-size unit suffixes beyond -h are out of scope for this release.\n  - LS_COLORS environment variable parsing is out of scope; we use GNU default colors only (prd003-format non-goals).\n  - ACL (access control list) display in long format (the \"+\" indicator) is out of scope.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: Default mode comparison -- gls and go run ./cmd/ls on a test directory produce identical multi-column output when run in a pseudo-TTY with the same terminal width. Differential test must set LC_ALL=C and --color=never.\"\n  - \"AC2: Single-column comparison -- gls -1 and go run ./cmd/ls -1 produce identical output on the same test directory.\"\n  - \"AC3: Long format comparison -- gls -l and go run ./cmd/ls -l produce identical output on the same test directory. Because mtime display depends on wall clock, differential tests must normalize the mtime column by replacing it with a fixed placeholder (e.g., \\\"MTIME\\\") before comparison. Because owner/group names may vary, tests must use a fixture where files are owned by the test user, or normalize owner/group fields.\"\n  - \"AC4: Color suppression -- gls --color=auto and go run ./cmd/ls --color=auto produce no ANSI escape sequences when stdout is piped (not a TTY).\"\n  - \"AC5: Human-readable sizes -- gls -lh and go run ./cmd/ls -lh produce identical size strings on a set of test files with known sizes (0 bytes, 1023 bytes, 1024 bytes, 1536 bytes, 1048576 bytes, 1073741824 bytes).\"\n  - \"AC6: Filter flags -- gls -a, gls -A, and gls -d produce identical output to the Go binary with the same flags on a test directory containing dotfiles and subdirectories.\"\n  - \"AC7: Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent path argument and exits 2 when given an invalid option, matching gls behavior.\"\n  - \"AC8: cmd/ls compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":137,"startLine":1,"totalLines":137}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WrTNwhzabDhU8mvBD5PaKq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture of what needs to be done. Let me plan the implementation:\n\n1. Introduce an `lsEntry` struct to pair entry names with their file type modes\n2. Add `--color` flag parsing\n3. Call `format.SetColorEnabled` based on the flag value\n4. Modify `collectEntries` to return `[]lsEntry` instead of `[]string`\n5. Modify `writeMultiColumn` and `writeSingleColumn` to apply color at render time\n6. Apply color to non-directory file arguments too\n\nLet me carefully modify `cmd/ls/main.go`.\n\nKey considerations:\n\n\n- I need to add the `--color` flag with `auto`, `always`, and `never` options, defaulting to `auto`\n- The color infrastructure from `pkg/format` will handle the actual color application\n- I'll create the `lsEntry` struct to hold both the entry name and its file type mode from `os.DirEntry.Type()`\n- Grid layout calculations will use plain names to keep spacing consistent, then color gets applied when rendering each entry\n- File arguments that aren't directories need color treatment too\n- Now I'm updating the function signatures: `collectEntries` and both column-writing functions need to work with `lsEntry` structs instead of plain strings\n- For the `--color` flag, I need to check how the existing code handles long flags since the standard `flag` package typically uses single-letter flags\n- The task specifies using `flag.String` with a default of `auto`, so I'll implement it that way rather than trying to handle the edge case from the PRD about `--color` without a value\n\nDefining the lsEntry struct... an entry name with its file type mode for color rendering.\n\nFor `collectEntries`, I need to get the file mode from `os.DirEntry.Type()`, and for `.` and `..` I'll use `os.ModeDir` since they're always directories. When rendering, I'll wrap each name with the appropriate color code based on its mode, and the color codes become empty strings when colors are disabled so nothing extra appears in the output.\n\nFor the multi-column layout, I'm thinking through whether to pass plain names to `format.Columns` for the grid calculation, then look up each entry's mode when rendering to apply color, or restructure to pass the full entries instead. I'm realizing that directory names are inherently unique, so a map lookup is safe. The tricky part is getting the padding right—I need to pad the plain name to the column width, then wrap it with color codes, not the other way around. Let me reconsider the rendering logic to make sure the color codes don't interfere with the width calculations. I need to reconsider the approach here. The requirements are clear: I should compute the grid layout using plain, uncolored names so that `format.Columns` and `format.PadRight` calculate widths correctly, then apply color wrapping at render time after padding. For non-final columns, I can wrap the padded text with color codes, and since the padding is just spaces, the reset code coming after them is fine. Alternatively, I could color the name first, then pad after the reset code...\n\nStill writing the column rendering logic... I see the approach now—padding the plain text first ensures the display width is correct, then wrapping that padded string with color codes so the spaces inherit the foreground color without any visual issues, and finally resetting after to keep subsequent entries clean. For the final column, I skip the padding and just apply color directly to the name before the newline.\n\nNow I'm refactoring the grid handling to separate layout computation from color rendering. Since the grid will contain plain names, I need a way to map those back to their file modes so I can apply the right colors when writing. I'm building a map that associates each name with its mode, then using that lookup when iterating through the grid to render each entry with the appropriate color.\n\nWriting the output logic... Writing the single column output function...\n\nNow I need to handle file arguments with color support too. Instead of just storing filenames as strings, I'll change `fileArgs` to store the full entry data including file mode, so I can apply the same color formatting when outputting individual files.\n\nFor the `collectEntries` function, I'm updating it to return `lsEntry` objects instead of plain strings...\n\nContinuing with the loop logic...\n\nNow I'm sorting the results by name and then handling the color flag configuration. I need to parse the color argument and set the appropriate formatting mode, with \"auto\" letting the format package detect TTY status automatically, while \"always\" and \"never\" explicitly control colorization. Invalid values should trigger an error message and exit.\n\nLooking at the implementation more carefully, I'm verifying that the variable names are consistent throughout—renaming `names` to `entries` in the collectEntries call and passing that through to writeMultiColumn so the types align properly.\n\nThe `showHeaders` logic should still work fine since it's just checking slice lengths, and the loop that sets `needSeparator` just needs the variable name updated from `name` to `e` when iterating over the fileArgs entries. I'm also double-checking that `os.Lstat` returns the correct file mode information for the file arguments being processed.\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a potential issue with how file modes are being handled across different sources. When I use `os.Lstat` on file arguments, I get the full mode with both type and permission bits, but when I use `os.DirEntry.Type()` in the directory listing, I only get the type bits without permission information. This matters for the `format.FileTypeColor` function, which checks things like whether a file is executable using `mode.Perm()&0o111 != 0`.\n\nThe problem is that `os.DirEntry.Type()` returns only the type portion of the mode, so executable files discovered through directory traversal would fail the executable check and display as regular files instead of getting the executable color. I need to use the full mode information from `os.DirEntry.Info()` instead of just `Type()` to properly detect executable permissions. So the constraint is that `DirEntry.Type()` only gives me type bits without permission information, which means I can't detect executable files to color them green. The task explicitly requires using `DirEntry.Type()` for the mode, but this creates a gap where executables would just render as regular files instead of getting their distinctive color. I need to decide if this limitation is acceptable given the requirements don't explicitly mandate executable coloring. so executable detection works for file arguments. For directory entries though, I'm weighing whether to strictly follow the task's requirement to use `DirEntry.Type()` and accept that executables won't be colored, or call `entry.Info()` to get the full permission bits. The acceptance criteria mention that output should match the file type colors defined in the color package, which includes executable coloring, but they don't explicitly test for it. Since the task specifically says to use `DirEntry.Type()`, I'll stick with that approach for now—if executable coloring becomes necessary, it can be added later.\n\nWait, I'm realizing there might be an issue with file arguments though.  For files passed as arguments, I'm already calling `os.Lstat` to get the full `os.FileMode`, which includes both type and permission bits—so `format.FileTypeColor` will correctly detect executables. But for directory entries collected via `DirEntry.Type()`, I only get the type bits, not the permission bits, so executable detection won't work there. That's acceptable since the task specifically requires using `DirEntry.Type()` for D2. And R5 is clear that I should use `os.Lstat` for file arguments anyway.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement the changes. I need to define an `lsEntry` struct to hold both the entry name and its color information, update `collectEntries` to return `[]lsEntry` instead of `[]string`, and switch from `sort.Strings` to `sort.Slice` to sort by the name field. Let me write out the modified code.\n\nWriting the updated main.go... I see that file arguments are always printed one per line in the current implementation, so I just need to wrap each one with color formatting rather than restructuring the output. Now I'm ready to write out the complete modified file with all the necessary changes - adding the struct definition, updating the flag handling, modifying the column functions to work with the new type, and applying color to the file argument output. I'm going to use targeted Edit calls to make these changes systematically. Let me start by adding the `lsEntry` struct, then handle the `--color` flag in main, update how file arguments are stored and processed to include file mode information, and finally modify the collection and output functions to work with the new structure. Adding the color flag parsing...\n\nNow I'm thinking through the logic for handling the color argument - setting the format module's color state based on whether it's \"always\", \"never\", or \"auto\" mode, with \"auto\" letting the package detect TTY status automatically, and erroring out on invalid values.\n\nFor the entry collection, I need to make sure the filtering logic correctly handles hidden files based on the show options. Refactoring the filtering and sorting logic...\n\nNow I'm applying these changes across multiple edit calls to update the function signatures and implementation details throughout the file.\n\nI should verify that the test file won't be affected by these changes since it relies on comparing stdout output, and the color behavior with `--color=auto` should remain consistent when piped through tests.\n\nNow I'm ready to start implementing the edits systematically, beginning with adding the new `lsEntry` struct and then updating the function signatures and implementations throughout the file.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1889,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4793,"cache_read_input_tokens":105863,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4793},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5db64dcf-77d9-43f9-86cc-418e9c0d9211","uuid":"126b41ed-29c4-4c62-827b-c5ff6242f5f6"}
